{#
	The dashboard picker (XIV-66), drawn identically on both screens that own a
	layout: your own on the account page, the installation's on the profile page.

	One partial rather than two, because they are the same list of the same widgets
	with the same arithmetic behind them — `App\Dashboard\SubmittedLayout` reads
	whatever this posts — and the only things that differ are which column the
	answer lands in, what "reset" means there, and which CSRF token the page around
	it uses.

	`panels` is `Dashboard::available()`: every widget that would draw something
	for the person looking at this page. A module the customer does not have and a
	module this reader has no grant on are both simply absent, because a widget
	that does not apply returns null and never produces a key (§6.2, §8.4). So the
	picker cannot offer a card that would come back empty, and nothing here has to
	check either of those things.

	A widget's name comes out of its own catalogue — `panel.domain` is `messages`
	for the application's own and the module's key for a module's — which is what
	lets `packages/invoice` name its card without touching the application's
	translation file.

	Parameters:
	  panels   array<string, WidgetPanel>  what is on offer, in declared order
	  chosen   list<string>|null           the layout in force here, or null for
	                                       "has not chosen"
	  action   string                      the `action` value the save posts
	  reset    string                      the `action` value the reset posts
	  inherit  string                      message key shown while nothing is chosen
	  token    string                      the CSRF token id this page validates
#}
{% if panels is empty %}
	{#
		Nothing to arrange. A newcomer with no modules and no grants still has the
		tiles, which never return null, so this is close to unreachable in practice
		— and is still worth drawing rather than leaving an empty form with a Save
		button under it.
	#}
	<p class="text-body-secondary mb-0">{{ 'dashboard.picker_empty'|trans }}</p>
{% else %}
	{#
		Where each kept widget currently sits, one-based, so the number boxes show
		the arrangement in force rather than the declaration order. Built here
		because it is a fact about what this form is drawing; the controllers hand
		over the layout itself and stay out of the presentation of it.
	#}
	{% set positions = {} %}
	{% for key in chosen ?? [] %}
		{% set positions = positions|merge({(key): loop.index}) %}
	{% endfor %}

	{% if chosen is null %}
		<p class="text-body-secondary small">{{ inherit|trans }}</p>
	{% endif %}

	<form method="post">
		<input type="hidden" name="_token" value="{{ csrf_token(token) }}">
		<input type="hidden" name="action" value="{{ action }}">

		<div class="list-group mb-3">
			{% for key, panel in panels %}
				<div class="list-group-item d-flex align-items-center gap-3">
					<div class="form-check flex-grow-1 mb-0">
						<input class="form-check-input" type="checkbox" id="widget_{{ action }}_{{ loop.index }}"
						       name="widgets[]" value="{{ key }}"
						       {{ (chosen is null or key in chosen) ? 'checked' : '' }}>
						<label class="form-check-label" for="widget_{{ action }}_{{ loop.index }}">
							{{ panel.nameKey|trans({}, panel.domain) }}
						</label>
					</div>

					{#
						Where it sits. A number rather than a drag handle: it works
						with the keyboard, needs no library, and survives a page of
						eight cards. Equal numbers keep the order the code declares,
						so somebody who ticks boxes and touches nothing else gets
						the arrangement they already had.
					#}
					<label class="visually-hidden" for="position_{{ action }}_{{ loop.index }}">
						{{ 'dashboard.picker_position'|trans }}
					</label>
					<input class="form-control form-control-sm widget-position" type="number" min="1" step="1"
					       id="position_{{ action }}_{{ loop.index }}"
					       name="positions[{{ key }}]"
					       value="{{ positions[key]|default(loop.index) }}">
				</div>
			{% endfor %}
		</div>

		<button class="btn btn-primary" type="submit">{{ 'common.save'|trans }}</button>
	</form>

	{#
		The way back, and its own form so that it cannot be confused with saving an
		empty selection — which is a different answer and one somebody may
		genuinely want. This is the escape the three-layer chain owes a person who
		has hidden everything: it needs no administrator, and on the account page
		it restores whatever the installation's default currently *is* rather than
		a copy of what it was when they last looked.
	#}
	<form method="post" class="mt-2">
		<input type="hidden" name="_token" value="{{ csrf_token(token) }}">
		<input type="hidden" name="action" value="{{ reset }}">
		<button class="btn btn-link btn-sm px-0" type="submit" {{ chosen is null ? 'disabled' : '' }}>
			{{ 'dashboard.picker_reset'|trans }}
		</button>
	</form>
{% endif %}
